#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110447 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9655010-b852-53ec-a6ae-f3fc95cc50cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle in Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "1a73bf50-94b6-536b-8213-eb3d0b3ea1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3349" :question/problem "![](images/69/69e1bae966baea90d6f2395bd4db263d3a564f53912f67d4e0812243d28550e3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta = 100^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane. What is its reference angle $\\theta_R$?" :question/worked-solution "The reference angle, denoted $\\theta_R$, is the angle that\n\n$$\n\\theta=100^\\circ\n$$\n\nmakes with the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/e4/e453e8a82b70d69009db59f161a798cb57c85602d79b4bcc44757cd2a192b860.png)\n\nSince $\\theta$ is in the second quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 180^{∘} - θ \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 100^{∘} \\\\\n&= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110448 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "174b9175-b7d6-5709-9db4-c3cc4f3834d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/id #uuid "36c616f3-67df-5833-90a6-81ee6966e1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3350" :question/problem "If the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{9}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way, then what is its reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{11\\pi} {9}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 220^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11\\pi} {9}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $220^\\circ$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n![](images/96/964e1116b6f9273321faf21f3f30c325ec81f49622eab951bc6a54823f3c7079.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted $\\theta_R$, is the angle that $\\theta$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta$ is in the third quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\theta - \\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\frac{11π}{9} - π = \\frac{2π}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110449 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8beabfbf-ac82-5bef-bec9-7f3ff980601c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "743ae44e-1a0c-5015-a292-f4e0f89cbfc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3352" :question/problem "![](images/75/75717526542bddffb22bbf8ca2593187fea84724fdd107b1327c049e6495a57b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta$ is measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $0.74$, calculate the reference angle $\\theta_R$ in degrees to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we create a right triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3cf6be5c87565683466ecf91646e3587accc41cd4d1774ec6aaed007f1e809.png)\n\nSo we have the following triangle, where $\\theta_R$ is the reference angle of $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/51/51bfc8624d27c386ceaaea38ab99ac1d96ed880e5674b90c9dd00b15579b0d6a.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle.\n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta_R = \\dfrac{0.74}{1} = 0.74\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\arccos\\left(0.74\\right) \\approx 42^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110450 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e10328e3-ff59-50d9-a3c3-ef145fe1bc16" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All of the Angles With a Given Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "591bf84d-62a7-5c9b-ada2-fdb2de62462b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3353" :question/problem "An angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 30^\\circ$. Which of the following cannot be $\\theta$?\n\n1. $30^\\circ$\n2. $150^\\circ$\n3. $240^\\circ$\n4. $330^\\circ$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between $\\theta_R$ and $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/57/57e85a4bfae7edc05e1328e4c275b6271bc9324fea64fd41d757a3de07f0a8c6.png)\n\nUsing\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 30^\\circ\n$$\n\nlet's calculate all possible values of $\\theta$.\n\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n$θ = θ_{R} = 30^{∘}$.\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n$θ|= 180^{∘} - θ_{R}; = 180^{∘} - 30^{∘}; = 150^{∘}$.\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n$θ|= 180^{∘} + θ_{R}; = 180^{∘} + 30^{∘}; = 210^{∘}$.\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n$θ|= 360^{∘} - θ_{R}; = 360^{∘} - 30^{∘}; = 330^{∘}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\theta$ cannot be equal to $240^\\circ$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8beabfbf-ac82-5bef-bec9-7f3ff980601c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "414a4b1b-25c9-54a2-b401-cd7e99615252" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108325}] :question/id #uuid "befe6288-c5bc-5cde-8210-b897555d39f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-266086" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c065ee38659ab3c3dc5b8b2997aa773b560bcf8971f5604421cf0bbe3eab73e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $θ$ measured in the usual way. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $0.91$, calculate the reference angle $θ_{R}$ to the nearest integer.$θ_{R} \\approx$\n{{field-1}} $^{∘}$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
